From 9802576209374bbdcdc7dc6379a9e10912c9094a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Valentin Haudiquet <valentin.haudiquet@canonical.com>
Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 23:20:37 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 15/21] arm: mediatek: mt8183: decode the previous bootloader's
 DRAM map

dram_init() reduced DRAM to a scalar base + size pair taken from the
control FDT (fdtdec_setup_mem_size_base()), which hardcodes 2 GiB for
all of kukui, and the weak default dram_init_banksize() turned that
into one contiguous bank.  On krane the boot chain is coreboot +
depthcharge, which own the real map -- 4 GiB of DRAM with regions
carved out for the firmware -- and none of that shape reached LMB and
the EFI memory map: every byte of the covered span was published as
conventional memory, carve-outs included.

The most damaging carve-out is the ARM Trusted Firmware TZRAM at
0x54600000: TF-A places BL31 there (plat/mediatek/mt8183 platform_def.h
TZRAM_BASE) and coreboot, which loads it, drops the 1 MiB region from
the memory it reports.  A kernel booted through U-Boot's EFI loader
instead reported the region as System RAM:

  52900000-bf5fffff : System RAM    <- via U-Boot: the hole is gone
  52900000-545fffff : System RAM    <- via depthcharge: 0x54600000 is
  54700000-...      : System RAM       absent from the map

Nothing touches it while the system idles (the kernel, its initrd and
its page tables land elsewhere), but stressapptest -M 3072 writes the
whole 3 GiB within seconds of starting and reaches the region: the
non-secure write takes an external abort, or corrupts the resident
BL31, whose next PSCI call (cpuidle, CPU hotplug) then wedges EL3.
Either way the machine silently hangs moments after stressapptest
prints "Starting SAT".

Fix the shape: decode the boot chain's map into gd->dram[] banks, one
bank per usable range, so LMB and the EFI memory map reproduce the same
holes instead of flattening them.

The ranges come from the coreboot tables, which are the authoritative
source: coreboot loaded BL31 and reserved the SCP and CBMEM regions,
maintains the tables in DRAM for the payload, and depthcharge's
/memory is itself just a copy of them.  The altfw payload ABI passes
the tables in x0; on the vboot kernel path they are found at the fixed
CBMEM address 0xffed9000.  The handoff FDT decode
(fdtdec_decode_ram_size() over /memory) is kept as a last-resort
fallback for previous bootloaders that only implement the Linux boot
ABI -- and must not be preferred: on the shipping depthcharge its
/memory reg reads back corrupt (a zero-size first range followed by
nonsensical ones) even though the FDT header validates, while the
tables read back reliably.

dram_init_banksize() keeps the decoded map; without one (control FDT
only, or no previous bootloader implementing either ABI) it falls back
to the default single contiguous bank.  CONFIG_NR_DRAM_BANKS grows
1 -> 8 to hold the ranges.  The boot log prints the source and the
decoded bank list, so a wrong map is visible instead of silent.

gd->ram_size stays the sum of the usable ranges, so the reported total
and the board_get_usable_ram_top() msdc 32-bit cap are unchanged.  The
carve-outs then simply do not exist in LMB or in the EFI memory map,
the same way they do not exist in the map depthcharge hands a kernel,
so the kernel sees them as the depthcharge boot does.

Signed-off-by: Valentin Haudiquet <valentin.haudiquet@canonical.com>
---
 arch/arm/lib/save_prev_bl_data.c     |  15 +-
 arch/arm/mach-mediatek/mt8183/init.c | 295 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 configs/mt8183_kukui_krane_defconfig |  43 +++-
 3 files changed, 337 insertions(+), 16 deletions(-)

